  completeExplanation: 'Chapter 4 of Ganita Prakash Grade 7 represents a cornerstone milestone in the middle school mathematics curriculum-the transition from concrete arithmetic to symbolic algebra. The chapter develops algebraic concepts intuitively through patterns, geometric representations, and authentic real-world contexts.\n\nThe conceptual journey begins with the concept of "letter-numbers"-letters used as symbols to represent numbers that can vary across different situations (variables). Students observe that if Shabnam is 3 years older than Aftab, Shabnam\'s age can be written as a + 3 when Aftab\'s age is a. This concise notation replaces endless specific arithmetic cases with a single general relationship.\n\nNext, standard algebraic grammar is established: in product expressions like 4 x n, the multiplication sign is omitted, yielding 4n, where the number (coefficient) precedes the letter. Students learn that substituting numbers for variables evaluates the expression to a numerical value. Common misconceptions, such as confusing concatenation (writing 36 instead of 3 x 6 = 18 for 3d when d = 6), are systematically unmasked and corrected.\n\nThe core of the chapter focuses on simplification and the structure of expressions. Terms containing identical letter-numbers are defined as "like terms" (e.g., 5c, 3c, 10c). By virtue of the distributive property, like terms can be combined: 5c + 3c + 10c = (5 + 3 + 10)c = 18c. Conversely, terms with different letter-numbers are "unlike terms" (e.g., 18c and 11d) and represent distinct independent quantities that cannot be combined.\n\nStudents then master bracket manipulation and sign rules. A minus sign preceding parentheses distributes to every term inside: -(18 + 13) = -18 - 13, and -(6x + 10y) = -6x - 10y. This enables the complete simplification of multi-variable expressions, such as quiz scores (7p - 3q) + (8p - 4q) + (6p - 2q) = 21p - 9q and comparative differences 23p - 7q - (21p - 9q) = 2p + 2q.\n\nFinally, the chapter demonstrates the explanatory and predictive power of algebra. Students explore "Formula Detective" number machines, saree border periodic designs categorized by remainder on division by 3 (3n - 2, 3n - 1, 3n), calendar invariants where any 2x2 square has equal diagonal sums (2a + 8) and any 3x3 cross sums to 5 times the center number (5a), matchstick chain progressions (triangles 2y + 1, squares 3w + 1), and grid coordinate formulas (N = 4(r - 1) + c). Through these rich investigations, algebra transforms from an abstract collection of symbols into an indispensable tool for mathematical proof and real-world modeling.',
